5. PHASE B — CRITICAL ARCHITECTURE FINDINGS TO VERIFY AND FIX

The uploaded repository contains evidence of the following issues. Confirm each one against the current files and fix the frontend impact.

5.1 Dual backend runtime and misleading compatibility names

Observed indicators include:

Plain Text


src/application/context/FirebaseContext.tsx
src/infrastructure/repositories/firebaseRepository.ts
src/infrastructure/repositories/index.ts
src/infrastructure/firebase/**
src/infrastructure/supabase/**
src/application/services/authService.ts



The repository selector appears to expose Supabase as the active repository, but Firebase modules are still imported and Firebase-specific storage services are still used by pages/services.

Fix requirements:

•
Identify the actual active adapter at runtime.

•
Ensure production frontend data operations use one explicit adapter.

•
Do not let Firebase modules initialize merely because a legacy class is imported.

•
Do not let pages call Firebase Storage when Supabase is the selected backend.

•
Replace misleading names gradually only where safe; compatibility aliases may remain temporarily, but comments must explain that they are migration aliases, not Firebase authority.

•
Add an explicit BackendAdapter or service boundary if the current structure allows it.

•
Make unsupported backend modes fail visibly instead of silently switching data sources.

•
Do not claim that Firebase has been removed if the repository still imports it.

If complete Supabase migration is intentionally deferred, document the temporary frontend boundary and ensure no page mixes Firebase and Supabase within one workflow.

5.2 LocalStorage is not an authoritative database

The uploaded project contains localStorage-backed repository behavior and active-user identifiers.

Fix requirements:

•
Do not use localStorage to prove authentication or authorization.

•
Do not treat a cached current-user ID as the active identity.

•
Do not display stale cached reservations, receipts, verification status, messages, or admin data as authoritative without a clear offline indicator.

•
In production mode, backend failures must produce an error state, not fake success or silent local persistence.

•
If an offline cache is retained, make it read-only/stale-aware until synchronized and clearly label it.

•
Clear user-specific cache on logout and account switch.

•
Namespace cache by authenticated user ID where safe.

•
Never cache sensitive documents, receipt contents, private messages, or signed URLs unnecessarily.

•
Keep mock/demo data behind an explicit test/development-only flag.

5.3 Configuration fallback risk

Inspect src/infrastructure/config/appConfig.ts and all environment handling.

Fix:

•
Do not use a fake localhost Supabase URL and fake anonymous JWT as a silent runtime fallback outside tests.

•
Do not allow production to start with missing or invalid configuration.

•
Use one canonical public key variable and document compatibility aliases.

•
Do not include service-role keys in Vite variables.

•
Do not import private Firebase configuration into the frontend merely to populate a compatibility object.

•
