Do not trust TypeScript types as runtime validation.

7.2 Safe error handling

Create or reuse a typed error-normalization utility that:

•
Returns user-safe messages.

•
Preserves a non-sensitive diagnostic code.

•
Does not expose tokens, SQL, stack traces, private URLs, or document contents.

•
Distinguishes unauthorized, forbidden, not found, validation, network, and unexpected errors.

7.3 Authorization-aware UI

Frontend checks are for usability only. Add comments near sensitive UI guards stating that backend/RLS enforcement is authoritative and still required.

Never hide security assumptions in a comment instead of enforcing them through the backend later.

7.4 Safe URL handling

•
Allow only internal application paths for post-login redirects.

•
Validate external directions/map URLs.

•
Do not interpolate untrusted values into href without validation.

•
Use rel="noopener noreferrer" for external new-tab links.

•
Do not store sensitive signed URLs in persistent state longer than necessary.

7.5 Safe uploads

Create a reusable client-side upload validation helper. It must:

•
Reject empty files.

•
Reject files above the MVP limit.

•
Reject unsupported MIME types.

•
Sanitize display names.

•
Avoid trusting extensions alone.

•
Avoid previewing untrusted HTML/SVG as active markup.

•
Revoke object URLs after use.

Document that client validation is not a replacement for Storage policies and backend validation.




